# Backend Philosophy

ranco-core is the primary backend for the MVP.

The backend architecture must prioritize:
- fast iteration
- simplicity
- realtime capabilities via WebSockets
- maintainability
- predictable data flows

Avoid:
- direct Supabase usage in the mobile app
- duplicated backend logic in the client
- complex event-driven systems in the app
- excessive client-side data access abstractions

# Backend Responsibilities (ranco-core)

ranco-core handles:
- authentication (JWT)
- database persistence
- WebSocket realtime events
- file storage uploads
- authorization (service layer)
- push notification dispatch

The mobile app communicates exclusively via HTTP REST and WebSockets.

# Repository Layer Rules

All API communication belongs inside repositories.

Good:

repositories/service-request.repository.ts

Avoid:
- direct HTTP calls in components or hooks
- Supabase client usage anywhere in the app
- duplicated API calls across services

Dependency flow:

```
hooks → services → repositories → src/services/api/client.ts
```

# Environment Variables

Use:

EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_WS_URL=

Never hardcode credentials or API URLs.

# Authentication Rules

Use JWT issued by ranco-core.

Rules:
- persist tokens securely (AsyncStorage via auth flow)
- centralize auth logic in auth service/repository
- protect authenticated routes
- attach `Authorization: Bearer <token>` on REST and WS connections
- avoid duplicated auth state

# API Response Envelope

All ranco-core responses follow:

```json
{
  "status": "success",
  "status_reason": "ok",
  "data": { ... }
}
```

Error responses use domain codes (`not_found`, `validation_error`, `unauthorized`, etc.) in `status`.

Repositories unwrap the envelope and throw typed errors for services to handle.

# Query Rules

- Keep repository methods simple and 1:1 with API endpoints
- Avoid duplicated query logic
- Select only required fields (via API query params when needed)
- Services own data transformation via mappers

# Storage Rules

Use ranco-core storage endpoints for:
- profile images
- service request photos
- chat media
- work evidence photos

Rules:
- validate uploads in services before sending
- use multipart upload via API client
- avoid oversized uploads unnecessarily

# WebSocket Rules

Use WebSockets for realtime where valuable.

Good realtime use cases:
- chat messages
- job status updates
- offer negotiation updates
- notifications
- typing indicators

Rules:
- connect with JWT on upgrade
- subscribe to scoped channels (`user:{id}`, `conversation:{id}`, `job:{id}`)
- clean up subscriptions on unmount
- prefer query invalidation over manual cache merging

Preferred integration:
- WebSocket event received
- `queryClient.invalidateQueries()` with matching query keys
- predictable cache updates

# Error Handling

- Always handle API errors in services
- Use user-friendly messages in UI
- Avoid exposing raw backend errors
- Keep async flows predictable

# MVP Philosophy

The client-backend contract should remain:
- simple
- reliable
- fast to iterate
- easy to debug

Prioritize:
- shipping product
- validating ideas
- maintaining velocity

Over:
- enterprise backend complexity
- premature scalability
- unnecessary infrastructure in the app

# Cursor AI Expectations

When generating API integration logic:
- Place HTTP calls in repositories only
- Keep services as orchestration layer
- Respect JWT auth flow
- Use WebSockets only where valuable
- Avoid Supabase imports or references
- Match ranco-core response envelope
